course: euphemisms
lesson: 32
title: Soft words for hard things: religion and fate and failure and mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When something goes badly, people often blame fate or luck instead of saying "it failed": "it wasn't meant to be" comforts a friend who lost a race, because it suggests nobody could have changed it.
explain: Many everyday phrases soften the word God, such as "goodness knows" or "for heaven's sake"; they are polite ways of saying "God knows" or "for God's sake".
explain: Some words are chosen to include everyone or to avoid naming one religion, like "happy holidays" instead of "Merry Christmas", or to describe someone's faith politely, like "lapsed".
explain: And some words spin a failure to sound better, such as calling a bad result "suboptimal" or a disaster "a blessing in disguise".
fact: "It wasn't meant to be" means it failed and you could not have changed it.
fact: "Everything happens for a reason" is said after something bad, to mean you should accept it.
fact: "A higher power" means God, said without naming any religion.
fact: "The good book" means the Bible.
fact: "Heaven forbid" means I really hope that does not happen.
fact: "Goodness knows" means God knows, that is, nobody knows.
fact: "Heaven knows" means God knows, that is, nobody knows.
fact: "For heaven's sake" is a polite way of saying "for God's sake", said in annoyance.
fact: "It's in God's hands" means nothing more can be done and we must wait and see.
fact: "A blessing in disguise" means a bad thing that may turn out to be good.
fact: "The luck of the draw" means it was chance, not your fault.
fact: "A man of the cloth" means a priest or clergyman, someone whose job is to lead a church.
fact: "Lapsed", as in "a lapsed Catholic", means no longer practising one's religion (following its prayers, services and rules).
fact: "Non-practising" means belonging to a religion but not following it.
fact: "Spiritual but not religious" means a person does not belong to or attend any organised religion.
fact: "Happy holidays" (mostly US) is a seasonal greeting that avoids naming Christmas.
fact: "A holiday tree" (US, official) means a Christmas tree, called by a name that avoids religion.
fact: "The festive season" (mostly UK) means Christmas and New Year.
fact: "Lady Luck" means chance or fortune, spoken of as if she were a person.
fact: "Suboptimal" (jargon) means bad or poor.
exercise: What does "it wasn't meant to be" mean after a team loses the final? | They lost and could not have changed it | They were never really playing in the final at all | They will win next week for sure | Someone cheated to make them lose
exercise: What does a friend mean by "everything happens for a reason" after your bike is stolen? | Something bad happened; accept it | The thief had a good reason to take it | You did something to deserve it | The bike will come back by itself
exercise: Who is "a higher power" in a talk about faith? | God | The boss of a company | A very tall person | The government
exercise: What is "the good book" when a preacher quotes it? | The Bible | A school dictionary | A very good novel | A cookery book
exercise: What does "if, heaven forbid, the bridge collapsed..." mean? | I really hope the bridge does not collapse | The bridge is not allowed in heaven | Heaven will stop the bridge collapsing | The bridge is sure to collapse
exercise: What does "goodness knows where he's gone" mean? | Nobody knows where he has gone | A good person knows where he is | He has gone somewhere good | He has gone to church
exercise: What does "heaven knows how she did it" mean? | Nobody knows how she did it | She did it in heaven | She did it with help from God | She did it in a very good way
exercise: What does "oh, for heaven's sake, sit down!" show about the speaker? | They are annoyed | They are praying | They are very happy | They are scared
exercise: The doctor says, "We've done all we can; it's in God's hands now." What does she mean? | Nothing more can be done; we must wait | God is doing the operation now | The patient has already got better | The patient must go to church
exercise: What does "missing the bus was a blessing in disguise" mean? | A bad thing turned out well | The bus was blessed by a priest | Someone in disguise drove the bus | Missing the bus was a sin
exercise: What does "it's the luck of the draw" mean about who gets the window seat? | It is chance, not anyone's fault | It is decided by a drawing contest | It is the luckiest seat on the bus | The best artist gets the seat
exercise: Who is "a man of the cloth"? | A priest or clergyman | A tailor who sews clothes | A man who sells curtains | A man who wears a cape
exercise: What does "she's a lapsed Catholic" mean? | She no longer practises her religion | She is always late for Mass on Sunday mornings | She fell over in church | She is a new Catholic
exercise: What does "my uncle is a non-practising Jew" mean? | He belongs to the religion but does not follow it | He has stopped practising the piano every day after school | He never trains for sport | He is learning to be a rabbi
exercise: What does "I'm spiritual but not religious" mean? | I do not belong to any organised religion | I believe in ghosts and spirits that visit the house | I go to church every day | I am a priest without a church
exercise: Why might a shop sign say "Happy Holidays" in the US? | To greet everyone without naming Christmas | Because it is the summer holidays | Because the shop is closed for a holiday over the weekend | To advertise trips abroad
exercise: What is a "holiday tree" in a US town square in December? | A Christmas tree | A tree planted on a holiday | A palm tree on a beach | A tree in a holiday camp
exercise: What does "the festive season" mean in Britain? | Christmas and New Year | The summer festival weeks | The harvest time in autumn | Any weekend with a party
exercise: What does "Lady Luck was on our side" mean? | We were lucky | A lady helped us win | A woman called Luck joined our team | We had a lucky charm
exercise: What does "the results were suboptimal" really mean? | The results were bad | The results were the best ever | The results were under the sea | The results were secret
test: A coach to a crying runner: "You trained so hard, love. It just wasn't meant to be." What is the coach saying? | You lost, but it was not your fault | You will win the next race for sure | You should stop running | You did not really try
test: Report from a company: "Sales this year were suboptimal." What really happened? | Sales were poor | Sales were excellent | Sales were exactly as planned | Sales stopped completely forever
test: "dunno where me keys are, heaven knows" — what does the speaker know? | They do not know where the keys are | The keys are in a church | The keys are upstairs | A neighbour round the corner has the keys
test: "Oh for heaven's sake, Tom, how many times do I have to tell ya!" How does the speaker feel? | Fed up and annoyed | Calm and kind | Sad and lonely | Excited and happy
test: Gossip: "Her brother's a man of the cloth now, would you believe." What is her brother's job? | He is a priest | He is a tailor | He sells carpets | He is a fashion model
test: Jake says: "'Lady Luck' is a real woman who gives out prizes, right?" Is he right? | No, it means chance or fortune | Yes, she runs the lottery | Yes, she is a famous singer | No, it is a horse's name
test: "I lost my job, but it was a blessing in disguise — now I run my own bakery." How did losing the job turn out? | Well in the end | Badly forever | It was all a disguise | Nobody knows yet
test: The teams were picked by names from a hat. "Bad team? That's the luck of the draw, mate." What is meant? | It was chance, not anyone's fault | You drew the worst picture | The captain chose you on purpose | You should have cheated
test: A council press release: "This year the holiday tree will be lit on 1 December." What will be lit? | A Christmas tree | A tree for the summer holiday | A bonfire | A palm tree
test: "Gran's in hospital, we've done everything, it's in God's hands now." What does the family mean? | They can only wait and hope | Gran is now with a priest | Gran has already recovered | The doctors have given up work
test: Two friends: "Do you go to church?" — "Nah, I'm a lapsed Methodist." What does the second friend mean? | They no longer practise their religion | They are a new Methodist | They are late for church | They lead the church choir every single Sunday
test: "If, heaven forbid, the dog got out, would ya know what to do?" What does "heaven forbid" show? | The speaker hopes the dog never gets out | The speaker is sure the dog will get out | Dogs are not allowed in heaven | The speaker wants the dog to get out
exam: It is given that a correspondent describes a relative as "spiritual but not religious". Which of the following statements most accurately characterises that relative? | The relative is not affiliated with any organised religion | The relative is an ordained member of the clergy | The relative professes belief exclusively in apparitions | The relative attends religious services daily
exam: Determine the meaning of the expression "suboptimal" as employed in respect of an organisation's annual results. | The results were unsatisfactory | The results exceeded all projections | The results were withheld from publication | The results were determined below sea level
exam: Whereas a speaker remarks that a misfortune "was a blessing in disguise", identify the principal meaning of the remark. | An apparently adverse event subsequently proved advantageous | The event was sanctified by a member of the clergy | The event was concealed from the speaker's family | The event constituted a grave moral transgression
exam: Ascertain the referent of the phrase "the good book" as customarily employed in English-speaking communities. | The Bible | A volume of exemplary moral fiction | A dictionary approved by the examination board | A ledger of charitable donations
